      "text": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a detailed internal engineering release plan for shipping a new authentication flow in a SaaS product. This should be written as an internal engineering document - practical, specific, technical, with clear ownership, timelines, and decision points. Not marketing fluff. Let me think about what makes a good internal release plan:\n\n1. It should have clear sections with ownership\n2. Specific technical details (not vague)\n3. Realistic timelines\n4. Concrete rollback criteria (not \"if things go wrong\")\n5. Feature flags with specific names and behavior\n6. Staged rollout with specific percentages and time gates\n7. Migration steps that are actually executable\n8. Monitoring with specific metrics and thresholds\n9. Customer communication that's practical (not \"we'll notify customers\")\n10. Security review with specific checklists\n11. Post-release verification with specific test cases\n\nLet me structure this as a real internal doc - with a header, metadata, and sections that an engineering team would actually use. I'll make it specific enough to be useful but general enough to apply to a SaaS product. I'll use concrete examples like specific flag names, specific metrics, specific time windows.\n\nLet me write this as a markdown document that looks like it belongs in an internal wiki or Confluence page.\n</think>\n\n# Release Plan: New Authentication Flow (Auth v2)\n\n**Doc owner:** Platform / Identity team\n**Status:** DRAFT \u2014 pending security sign-off\n**Target ship date:** 2025-07-14 (Mon)\n**Affected services:** `auth-service`, `api-gateway`, `session-store`, `admin-portal`, `mobile-sdk`\n**Affected data stores:** `users` table, `sessions` table, `auth_tokens` table\n**Blast radius:** All authenticated endpoints. Unauthenticated public endpoints unaffected.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nWe are replacing the legacy JWT-only auth flow (single 24h token, no refresh, no MFA) with a new flow that supports:\n\n- Short-lived access tokens (15 min) + rotating refresh tokens (30 days, single-use)\n- Optional TOTP / WebAuthn MFA\n- Device-bound sessions with revocation\n- OAuth 2.0 / OIDC for the admin portal (replacing the current basic-auth shim)\n\nThis is a **breaking change at the protocol level** but must be **non",
      "text_preview": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a detailed internal engineering release plan for shipping a new authentication flow in a SaaS product. This should be written as an internal engineering document - practical, specific, technical, with clear ownership, timelines, and decision points. Not marketing fluff. Let me think about what ",
